# Cairn re-anchor ceremony -- values file (TEMPLATE)
#
# Copy this file OUTSIDE the repository, fill in every value, and pass the copy
# to the launcher, which is the only reader of it: it checks every line, then
# hands the ceremony artifact the thirteen values. It is mutable operational
# configuration, not authority: it carries no secret material and no authority value. The reviewed
# candidate OID and the ceremony artifact's digest deliberately are NOT here --
# they arrive through the reviewed launcher, because a value that decides what
# is trusted must not sit in a file the ceremony itself invites you to edit.
#
# EDITING THIS FILE CAN REDIRECT WHAT THE CEREMONY READS. That sits inside
# Cairn's accepted same-user residual and is not closed here. File ownership and
# mode are hygiene; they do not distinguish you from an agent running as the
# same OS user.
#
# GRAMMAR (refusals are fail-closed, and there is no ambient fallback of any
# kind -- an absent field refuses rather than being filled from PATH, HOME or
# Git config):
#
#   * one record per line: the key, exactly ONE space, then the value verbatim
#     to end of line
#   * no leading whitespace on any line; `#` is a comment only at column 0
#   * trailing whitespace REFUSES rather than being trimmed -- trimming would
#     silently change a path
#   * no control characters; LF line endings; max 4096 bytes per line and
#     64 KiB per file
#   * every path is absolute, and no component of it may be a symbolic link
#   * unknown key, duplicate key, missing key, wrong file type or a missing
#     executable bit all refuse
#
# RECORDING THE EXECUTABLE PATHS, AND REFRESHING THEM. Record the RESOLVED
# REGULAR FILE, not a convenience symlink: on a Homebrew host
# /opt/homebrew/bin/git is a symlink into a version-pinned Cellar path, and this
# schema refuses it. Resolve it once (for example with `readlink -f`) and record
# what comes out. That also means the entry GOES STALE at the next toolchain
# upgrade, when the Cellar path changes -- so run the launcher with `--validate`
# after any upgrade of git, python or the coreutils. The refusal you get is correct and
# is not a reason to loosen the rule.

# The exact schema token the launcher reads. Any other value refuses.
schema cairn-ceremony-values/v1

# The repository being blessed. Must be a worktree TOP LEVEL: `git -C`
# discovers upward, so a path beneath a repository would bless one never named.
checkout /absolute/path/to/the/checkout

# The ceremony signing key's PUBLIC half. Stage 1 probes this channel and
# refuses if it finds it can sign; stage 2 signs with it. This is the key whose
# fingerprint must appear in dev/.cairn/allowed_signers.
signing_key /absolute/path/to/cairn_signing.pub

# Where the authorization package is written by stage 1 and read by stage 2.
# The leaf MUST NOT exist at stage 1 and MUST exist at stage 2; the parent must
# be an existing, symlink-free directory. Keep it OUTSIDE the checkout: a
# package written into the tree becomes part of the state it authorizes.
package /absolute/path/to/authorization-package.json

# HOME for the signing backend. Recorded rather than inherited.
home /absolute/path/to/your/home

# --- executables this ceremony runs -------------------------------------
# Each is invoked by absolute path, which no inherited shell function or alias
# can shadow. Each must be an existing, symlink-free, executable regular file.
env /usr/bin/env
git /absolute/path/to/git
python /absolute/path/to/python3.13
mktemp /usr/bin/mktemp
mkdir /bin/mkdir
shasum /usr/bin/shasum
sort /usr/bin/sort

# `cat` is here for the same reason the others are. The launcher snapshots the
# authorization packet through it before parsing, so it is executed -- and an
# executable that is trusted but unbound can only be found through ambient
# PATH, which is exactly the gap the recorded paths above close.
cat /bin/cat
